{"step_index":1182,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T23:33:57Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nshow proofs to the numbers you gave.  \"Ninety-eight percent on fifty pairs is a strong signal, but the sample is small enough that the confidence interval is wide — you're looking at roughly a ninety-three to ninety-nine percent range at ninety-five percent confidence. That's enough to greenlight the next cycle, not enough to declare the corpus clean. The 2.94 ns per insertion is the number that actually changes the picture. That's not \"fast for a memory allocator\" — that's faster than a single L1 cache hit on the M4, which sits around 3 to 4 ns. You cannot physically perform 100,000 pointer writes, bounds checks, and arena bookkeeping in 2.94 ns per operation. The only way that number is real is if the compiler saw the loop as dead and erased it, or the timed region captured almost nothing.\n\nThis is exactly the elision I flagged. The black-box readback I described — a checksum consumed outside the timed section — is no longer optional. It's the difference between a 294-tick result and a 294-tick artifact. Run it, and if the number survives, you've got something genuinely remarkable. If it doesn't, you've learned the harness was lying to you, which is worth more than the fake number was.\n\nOn the PMU profiling — yes, and specifically you want the L2C_CACHE_MISS_REMOTE event or its Apple Silicon equivalent, not just a general cache-miss counter. False sharing shows up as remote cache misses, not local ones. A local miss could be normal working-set pressure. A remote miss is two cores fighting over the same line. That's the signal that either vindicates or buries the zero-contention claim.\n\nOne more thing on the DPO validation: 98% agreement is good, but agreement between what and what? If it's the same model scoring its own pairs, that's not validation — that's the echo chamber grading its own homework. The scorer needs to be independent of the generator. A separate model, a human, or at minimum a different checkpoint. Otherwise you've measured self-consistency, not quality.\"\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T19:33:57-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
